gGoals":["Subject and object","Before a noun","Replacing a noun phrase"],"guidance":"Read the short rules and examples, then complete a 20-question session. Use feedback to explain each choice. Revisit the skill you missed before trying again.","rules":[{"title":"Subject and object","explanation":"Use I, he, she, we or they as subjects. Use me, him, her, us or them as objects. You and it keep the same form.","examples":["She invited us.","They spoke to me."]},{"title":"Before a noun","explanation":"Use my, your, his, her, its, our or their directly before a noun. These determiners identify the owner.","examples":["Our appointment is tomorrow.","Her parcel has arrived."]},{"title":"Replacing a noun phrase","explanation":"Mine, yours, his, hers, ours and theirs stand on their own. Do not add an apostrophe. His works both before a noun and on its own.","examples":["This seat is yours.","Those gloves are theirs."]}],"tips":["Read the full context before choosing.","Check the explanation, including after a correct answer.","A passing practice session is useful feedback, not a language certificate."],"source":"seed","provenance":{"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","authorship":"Grammacho; generated offline with AI assistance","kind":"original-guidance-aligned","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending","referenceIds":["bc-pronouns","bc-possessives","pcc-esol"],"assessment":"Practice only; levels are approximate teaching labels, not validated CEFR assessment."}}'::jsonb j) payload
